  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1115 AM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nAfter a chilly start to our day, especially in areas east of the\nI-55 corridor, temperatures have begun to warm into the 50s under\nmostly clear skies. A broad surface high pressure, encompassing\nthe Midwest and into the Mid-South, continues to dominate the\nweather pattern. Taking a look at the 12Z sounding from Little\nRock, Arkansas, no moisture is present through the entire column.\nAs such, dry weather will persist with current and afternoon\ndewpoints largely in the lower 30s. NBM continues to overdo\ndewpoints, especially in this cool, dry airmass, therefore,\nareawide dewpoints were lowered several degrees. As winds go\nlight and calm overnight along narrow dewpoint depressions, low-\nlying areas could see some patchy fog. Widespread fog is not\nanticipated as forecast soundings have us very dry in the lower-\nlevels and down to the surface. Another frigid morning is\nforecast Monday with NBM and REFS probabilities hinting at a >50%\nchance of below freezing temperatures mainly east of I-55.\n\nMoving into the workweek, a warming trend will begin as a 585dam\nridge begins to build into the Middle Mississippi Valley from the\nSouthern Plains. This upper ridge will drive the weather across\nour area through at least Saturday with afternoon highs creeping\ninto the 70s by Thursday. We'll wake up to chilly mornings in the\n30s and 40s through midweek and mainly in the 40s by late week\nand into the weekend. Early morning frost is anticipated each day\nthrough midweek, especially east of the Mississippi River. As far\nas precipitation goes over the next seven days, the Weather\nPrediction Center has us highlighted with 0\" of rainfall as the\naforementioned ridge remains parked over the region. There does\nlook to be a glimmer of light at the end of the tunnel to start\nnext week as ensemble members are hinting at an upper low pushing\ninto the upper Great Lakes Region with an attendant cold front\npushing across our forecast area. The question remains how much\nmoisture will be associated with this next system. For now we\nwill wait and see. Drought conditions continue to worsen with the\nlatest drought monitor displaying Extreme (D3) to Exceptional\n(D4) drought in areas west of the Mississippi River and portions\nof north Mississippi with Moderate (D1) to Severe (D2) Drought\nacross the rest of Mid-South.\n\nAEH",
